course: dictionary
lesson: 7301
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word pit is a good example. «The children dug a pit in the sand» means a hole in the ground. «The peach has a large pit in the middle» means a hard stone, not a hole at all.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The car pulled into the pits for new tyres» is about motor racing: the pits are the place beside the track where cars stop. «My bedroom is a pit!» is slang for a very messy room. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: Pit can also be a verb. «The coach pitted the two teams against each other» means made them compete. To check your choice, put your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: Many common words have more than one meaning.
fact: «Pit» can mean a large hole in the ground, dug by people or made naturally.
fact: In the UK, a pit can be a coal mine, where miners dig out coal.
fact: In the US and Canada, a pit is the hard stone in the middle of some fruits, like a peach or cherry.
fact: «The pit of your stomach» means the deep inside of your belly, where you feel fear.
exercise: «Badgers dig special toilet pits away from the sett.» What is a pit in this sentence? | A coal mine in the UK | A hard stone in a fruit | A place where racing cars stop | *A large hole in the ground
exercise: «The peach had a large pit in the middle.» What does pit mean here? | *A hard stone in the middle of a fruit | A deep hole in the ground | To make two people compete | A very messy room
exercise: «The cars came into the pits for new tyres.» What are the pits in this sentence? | The seats in the stands | *The place beside the track where cars stop | A dark, dusty trap | A deep hole where miners work
test: «Joseph's brothers threw him into a pit.» In this sentence, pit means | The hard stone of a fruit | *A deep hole in the ground | A messy room | To make people compete
test: Mia says, «To pit Sam against Ben is to make them compete.» What does pit mean as a verb? | To dig a hole in the ground | To clean up a messy room | To feel sick with fear | *To make two people compete or fight
exam: «I had a sinking feeling in the pit of my stomach before the test.» What does pit mean in this phrase? | *The deep inside of your belly, where you feel fear | A coal mine in the UK | A large hole in the ground | The hard stone of a fruit
